1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:00,880
Three observers.

2
00:00:01,240 --> 00:00:01,830
One target.

3
00:00:02,540 --> 00:00:03,369
One sees it now.

4
00:00:03,780 --> 00:00:05,280
One searches the immediate past.

5
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:07,110
One searches the immediate future.

6
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:10,440
Their descriptions are compared only after
the session.

7
00:00:11,030 --> 00:00:13,100
This was not written for a science-fiction
film.

8
00:00:13,480 --> 00:00:17,290
It is Recommendation H in a report
prepared for the United States Army.

9
00:00:17,900 --> 00:00:19,510
June ninth, 1983.

10
00:00:19,980 --> 00:00:22,260
Written by Lieutenant Colonel Wayne M.
McDonnell.

11
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:23,850
Fort Meade, Maryland.

12
00:00:24,370 --> 00:00:27,770
The declassified copy now carries CIA
archive release marks.

13
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:31,240
That is why the internet calls it the CIA
Gateway Report.

14
00:00:31,860 --> 00:00:35,200
But this was an Army analysis, not a CIA-
authored report.

15
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:39,200
And Recommendation H was not McDonnell's
strangest proposal.

16
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:45,760
He also wanted personnel prepared for
possible encounters with intelligent, non-
corporeal energy forms.

17
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:47,840
How did an intelligence officer arrive
there?

18
00:00:48,380 --> 00:00:51,140
The path begins with two tones in a pair
of headphones.

19
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:54,010
It ends with time treated like a military
coordinate.

20
00:00:54,460 --> 00:00:56,820
And hidden in the middle is a test with
ten digits.

21
00:00:57,280 --> 00:00:59,320
The result was too weak to prove the
claim.

22
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:02,340
And, to McDonnell, interesting enough not
to discard.

23
00:01:02,910 --> 00:01:05,160
The first man in the chain was Robert
Monroe.

24
00:01:05,740 --> 00:01:08,830
Monroe had built a successful career in
radio broadcasting.

25
00:01:09,880 --> 00:01:15,280
Then he began describing episodes in which
he seemed to observe his own body from
outside it.

26
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:16,940
He did not respond like a mystic.

27
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:19,540
He responded like an engineer.

28
00:01:19,580 --> 00:01:20,570
He built a laboratory.

29
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:28,520
At the Monroe Institute in Virginia,
recordings, spoken instructions, and
numbered mental states became a training
system.

30
00:01:28,580 --> 00:01:30,000
Monroe called it Hemi-Sync.

31
00:01:30,620 --> 00:01:34,340
McDonnell was asked to assess how it might
work and whether it could be useful.

32
00:01:35,220 --> 00:01:39,240
To build an explanation, he turned to a
man who had already been dead for four
years.

33
00:01:39,790 --> 00:01:41,480
Itzhak Bentov was an inventor.

34
00:01:42,120 --> 00:01:44,940
His work contributed to the steerable
cardiac catheter.

35
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:49,440
He also believed the human body behaved
like an oscillating system.

36
00:01:49,460 --> 00:01:51,300
The heart and aorta created rhythm.

37
00:01:51,870 --> 00:01:53,210
The body entered resonance.

38
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:58,880
And consciousness, he proposed, could ride
that resonance into a larger structure of
reality.

39
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:04,760
On May twenty-fifth, 1979, Bentov boarded
American Airlines Flight 191 in Chicago.

40
00:02:05,260 --> 00:02:06,880
The flight was bound for Los Angeles.

41
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:10,840
Moments after takeoff, the aircraft lost
an engine and crashed.

42
00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:14,880
Four years later, his model reappeared
inside an Army analysis.

43
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:16,829
Monroe supplied the training.

44
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:19,410
Bentov supplied the proposed physics.

45
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:20,900
McDonnell connected them.

46
00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:24,260
At first, the mechanism is real and
modest.

47
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:26,800
Play four hundred hertz in the left ear.

48
00:02:27,420 --> 00:02:28,840
Four hundred and ten in the right.

49
00:02:29,420 --> 00:02:32,420
The listener perceives a pulse of roughly
ten beats per second.

50
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:35,680
No speaker produces that pulse directly.

51
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:39,520
It emerges as the auditory system
processes the difference between the
tones.

52
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:41,440
That is a binaural beat.

53
00:02:42,040 --> 00:02:46,150
Monroe proposed that these beats could
help guide the brain toward particular
patterns.

54
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:51,680
If the hemispheres became more coherent,
perhaps unusual states became easier to
reach.

55
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:56,720
Monroe had patented a related sound-based
sleep technique in 1975.

56
00:02:57,360 --> 00:03:03,040
Up to this point, the document is talking
about sound, attention, electrical
activity, and altered state.

57
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:05,180
Then the report changes scale.

58
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:06,420
Watch the leap.

59
00:03:06,940 --> 00:03:07,900
The body oscillates.

60
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:10,160
The brain produces electrical patterns.

61
00:03:10,740 --> 00:03:13,200
Synchronization makes the patterns more
coherent.

62
00:03:13,820 --> 00:03:23,360
And then, McDonnell proposes, sufficiently
coherent consciousness may connect with a
field of information in which space and
time do not behave as they do in ordinary
perception.

63
00:03:23,930 --> 00:03:25,700
The page still looks like engineering.

64
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:26,580
Arrows.

65
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:27,370
Axes.

66
00:03:27,700 --> 00:03:28,290
Vortices.

67
00:03:28,680 --> 00:03:29,590
Nested systems.

68
00:03:29,970 --> 00:03:30,700
Army letterhead.

69
00:03:31,340 --> 00:03:34,130
But the report has crossed from acoustics
into cosmology.

70
00:03:35,180 --> 00:03:37,040
There are three ways to read this
crossing.

71
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:42,060
One: McDonnell built a speculative
metaphor and began treating it like a
mechanism.

72
00:03:42,740 --> 00:03:48,660
Two: the language reflects a military era
willing to investigate any possible
intelligence advantage.

73
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:55,040
Three: unusual experiences persisted often
enough that he believed the model deserved
a test.

74
00:03:55,570 --> 00:03:58,460
The document does not tell us which
interpretation is correct.

75
00:03:59,160 --> 00:04:00,700
It does show what he did next.

76
00:04:01,320 --> 00:04:01,990
He drew a map.

77
00:04:02,660 --> 00:04:03,329
Focus 10.

78
00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:04,269
Mind awake.

79
00:04:04,580 --> 00:04:05,269
Body asleep.

80
00:04:05,900 --> 00:04:06,650
Focus 12.

81
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:08,900
Awareness beyond the ordinary senses.

82
00:04:09,340 --> 00:04:11,360
Then comes the heading that changes the
file.

83
00:04:12,220 --> 00:04:13,140
Focus 15.

84
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:14,560
Travel into the Past.

85
00:04:15,180 --> 00:04:17,380
McDonnell imagines time as a giant wheel.

86
00:04:17,899 --> 00:04:18,920
The hub is the present.

87
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:22,000
The spokes extend toward points in a
person's past.

88
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:25,560
He also admits that the state is extremely
difficult to reach.

89
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:31,740
McDonnell wrote that fewer than five
percent of participants in any given
Gateway training achieved it fully.

90
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:33,780
Then comes Focus 21.

91
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:34,830
The Future.

92
00:04:35,240 --> 00:04:39,180
Immediately afterward, the report gives
physical instructions for leaving the
body.

93
00:04:39,840 --> 00:04:40,440
Roll out.

94
00:04:40,820 --> 00:04:41,430
Rise upward.

95
00:04:41,930 --> 00:04:42,950
Move through an opening.

96
00:04:43,360 --> 00:04:46,440
The language reads like a field manual for
an impossible maneuver.

97
00:04:47,540 --> 00:04:50,820
But an intelligence officer eventually has
to ask a harder question.

98
00:04:51,440 --> 00:04:52,720
Can it retrieve information?

99
00:04:53,340 --> 00:04:54,900
This is where the ten digits appear.

100
00:04:55,260 --> 00:04:58,220
A computer-generated sequence is placed at
another location.

101
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:00,080
Nobody in the room knows it.

102
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:03,620
Participants are supposed to read the
sequence while out of body.

103
00:05:04,260 --> 00:05:11,660
According to a Monroe Institute trainer
cited by McDonnell, some obtained enough
matching digits to convince him their
attention had reached the lab.

104
00:05:12,280 --> 00:05:13,680
Nobody obtains all ten.

105
00:05:14,220 --> 00:05:16,090
That result is the center of the episode.

106
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:17,870
Not a clean success.

107
00:05:18,420 --> 00:05:19,710
Not a clean demonstration.

108
00:05:20,330 --> 00:05:22,660
Just enough resemblance to keep the report
moving.

109
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:24,780
McDonnell blames distortion.

110
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:29,840
Impressions from the target, the present
environment, and other moments supposedly
blend together.

111
00:05:30,640 --> 00:05:32,720
The observer cannot tell what belongs
where.

112
00:05:33,140 --> 00:05:36,160
And the failed result becomes the design
for Recommendation H.

113
00:05:36,920 --> 00:05:37,840
Would you authorize it?

114
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:39,660
One observer in the present.

115
00:05:40,140 --> 00:05:41,360
One in the immediate past.

116
00:05:41,820 --> 00:05:42,990
One in the immediate future.

117
00:05:43,820 --> 00:05:49,150
If all three describe the same thing,
McDonnell hopes agreement will isolate the
target from the distortion.

118
00:05:49,900 --> 00:05:50,920
Authorize the test?

119
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:52,520
Or stop the program here?

120
00:05:53,310 --> 00:05:55,390
Leave one word: AUTHORIZE or STOP.

121
00:05:56,120 --> 00:05:57,230
McDonnell did not stop.

122
00:05:58,260 --> 00:06:07,250
Recommendation J says participants should
be intellectually prepared for possible
encounters with intelligent, non-corporeal
energy forms beyond ordinary space-time.

123
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:17,810
Recommendation K proposes that groups in
Focus 12 build holographic patterns around
sensitive areas to repel possible unwanted
out-of-body presences.

124
00:06:17,840 --> 00:06:21,270
The report is no longer merely asking
whether headphones change attention.

125
00:06:21,920 --> 00:06:26,510
It is outlining a defensive doctrine for a
space whose existence has not been
established.

126
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:30,400
Decades later, the modest part of the
chain remains testable.

127
00:06:30,940 --> 00:06:36,020
Studies of binaural beats have examined
attention, anxiety, pain, and brain
activity.

128
00:06:36,640 --> 00:06:38,640
Results vary by method and outcome.

129
00:06:39,400 --> 00:06:41,880
Some reviews find modest psychological
effects.

130
00:06:42,720 --> 00:06:46,520
Evidence that the brain reliably locks to
the intended frequency is mixed.

131
00:06:47,140 --> 00:06:51,420
Even if the sound changes a mental state,
the central evidentiary gap remains.

132
00:06:52,220 --> 00:06:55,070
Feeling different is not evidence of
reading hidden digits.

133
00:06:55,720 --> 00:06:59,000
The report provides no controlled dataset
that crosses that gap.

134
00:06:59,460 --> 00:07:00,330
So what survives?

135
00:07:00,980 --> 00:07:02,210
The document is authentic.

136
00:07:02,580 --> 00:07:06,640
Its author, date, recipient, and
recommendations are visible on the page.

137
00:07:07,300 --> 00:07:09,620
Travel into the Past is there.

138
00:07:09,660 --> 00:07:11,920
The incomplete ten-digit result is there.

139
00:07:12,340 --> 00:07:14,400
The nonphysical intelligences are there.

140
00:07:15,100 --> 00:07:19,350
What is not there is proof that Gateway
opened a path outside space or time.

141
00:07:20,040 --> 00:07:22,760
The viral claim says the CIA proved it
worked.

142
00:07:23,140 --> 00:07:25,580
The real document tells a more unsettling
story.

143
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:33,560
In 1983, an Army officer borrowed an audio
system, a dead inventor's physical model,
and a collection of anomalous experiences.

144
00:07:34,160 --> 00:07:37,600
Then he built a test plan for observers in
three different times.

145
00:07:38,160 --> 00:07:39,800
Was that bureaucratic overreach?

146
00:07:40,180 --> 00:07:41,470
Cold War desperation?

147
00:07:41,860 --> 00:07:44,660
Or an unanswered anomaly wrapped in the
wrong theory?

148
00:07:45,360 --> 00:07:47,170
The report cannot settle that question.

149
00:07:47,540 --> 00:07:52,130
But the next experiment removes the
visions, the entities, and the military
language.

150
00:07:52,720 --> 00:07:54,820
It replaces them with zeros and ones.

151
00:07:55,480 --> 00:07:56,730
Fourteen million trials.

152
00:07:57,220 --> 00:08:00,740
And one unnamed person responsible for
roughly half the effect.
